<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">March of Verona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">952</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March of Verona was a frontier territory in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, created by Otto I in 952 from lands around Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> and given to his brother Henry I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It controlled the Alpine routes into Italy along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a>. In 976 it was attached to the new Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, and its authority faded as the Italian cities gained self-government in the twelfth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-of-verona.jpg" alt="March of Verona" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: New Marco · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_March_of_Verona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
